    40	# Codex Adversarial Review
    41	
    42	Target: working tree diff
    43	Verdict: needs-attention
    44	
    45	Do not ship as an executable research proposal. The forecast accumulates drift in the wrong units, the dose-response hypothesis contradicts its own equation, and the protection study cannot independently verify the claimed guarantee.
    46	
    47	Findings:
    48	- [high] The forecast adds per-candidate drift once per generation (docs/idea-summaries/06-who-self-improvement-leaves-behind.md:124-132)
    49	  The drift log divides the selected gains by K, then the forecast adds a per-proposal gain once per generation. With four candidates and one winner, the logged value is one quarter of the actual guard-set change for that round. In the DGM-style archive, the next proposal can also start from an older parent, so summing edges in generation order need not recover the final harness's change from the seed.
    50	  Recommendation: Define a per-generation change for a specified incumbent, include the best-of-K acceptance probability, and verify that accumulated logged changes match directly scored endpoints. For archive runs, track the reported incumbent or restrict the forecast to its lineage.
    51	- [high] Tail sample size can reverse the predicted sign (docs/idea-summaries/06-who-self-improvement-leaves-behind.md:94-100)
    52	  Equation 96 adds a mean-effect term to the covariance term. Noise can change which dominates. With Figure 8's spreads, correlation and noise variance, but mean effects 0.20 and −0.18 at tail weight 0.40, the proposal's own equation gives drift of −0.010 at 16 tail items and +0.028 at 256. H2 would count a result permitted by the model as a falsification. The same distinction applies to the best-of-K claim about total drift.
    53	  Recommendation: State sign invariance for the covariance contribution under the stated noise assumptions. Preregister predictions for total drift using both terms, and label Figure 8's zero-mean, single-proposal case explicitly.
    54	- [high] StaBLE uses its test participants to choose the certified checkpoint (docs/idea-summaries/06-who-self-improvement-leaves-behind.md:165-172)
    55	  The pseudocode certifies checkpoints on T, and Figure 6's caption gives those same test people both audit and certification roles. A checkpoint can pass because its measured tail error was unusually low on those people. Reusing that estimate for the certified arm's protection frontier gives a selected estimate, even if fixed-sequence testing controls false certification.
    56	  Recommendation: Freeze checkpoints before certification and evaluate the checkpoint selected for deployment on an independent audit cohort. Specify which participant blocks serve each role and keep fold-specific artifacts and outcomes isolated.
    57	- [high] The StaBLE certification protocol has no specified valid test (docs/idea-summaries/06-who-self-improvement-leaves-behind.md:236-238)
    58	  A sealed sample preserves independence, but the draft's own skew example invalidates its t-test. The proposed fallback requires bounded loss, while held-out target scores are explicitly unclipped and the protocol supplies no loss bound. Power is also a separate blocker. Even with a one-unit upper bound on paired harm, a margin-null population with harm 1 for 3% of people produces five zero differences 86% of the time. The StaBLE paper reports 25 fall-risk participants in the full cohort, so there are about five per block at best on average. [StaBLE source, Figure 5](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13621976/)
    59	  Recommendation: Preregister an explicitly bounded loss, a finite-sample valid test, and the expected certification rate at the actual eligible tail count. Treat StaBLE certification as exploratory if the study cannot certify practically useful checkpoints.
    60	- [high] The dose-response grid cannot isolate the proposed selection mechanism (docs/idea-summaries/06-who-self-improvement-leaves-behind.md:217-220)
    61	  E4 lets each weight and sample-size cell evolve its own parent and uses covariance from five E1 lineages to predict all cells. Development data also supply proposer traces. Changing the sample size can therefore change the evidence shown to the proposer and the edits it generates. A sign change could come from a different candidate distribution, even if rescoring the same candidates never changes the sign.
    62	  Recommendation: Cross weights and sample sizes on identical frozen parent–candidate batches with fixed proposer feedback. Sample by question ID, account for dependence across translations, and test the resulting local predictions separately from the full-loop effects.
    63	- [high] The protection experiment cannot validate error control or the sizing rule (docs/idea-summaries/06-who-self-improvement-leaves-behind.md:218-222)
    64	  Global-MMLU allocates ten runs per rule at one tail sample size, 64. Even zero violations in ten independent runs gives a one-sided 95% upper bound of about 26%, far above the claimed 5%. The sample-size sweep uses the unprotected mean gate, so it cannot validate the protection sizing rule. One fresh 64-item test per generation already consumes 3,200 distinct question IDs per run. The plan budgets no independent reference sample for deciding whether a run truly crossed the margin.
    65	  Recommendation: Keep theoretical error control conditional on a specified valid test. Allocate fresh gate and independent reference pools by question ID, sweep sample size for the protective gate itself, and report uncertainty on violation rates.
    66	- [medium] The novelty test does not isolate the value of rejected edits (docs/idea-summaries/06-who-self-improvement-leaves-behind.md:55-68)
    67	  Hu and Tang already scored all candidates on a separate audit set and used early candidate logs to predict held-out winner effects. The defensible extension is a prospective long-horizon subgroup forecast. E3 compares the proposed model with different forecasting rules, so a win could come from its trajectory model or majority signal without any contribution from rejected edits. [Hu and Tang, Sections 4 and 6](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.09239)
    68	  Recommendation: Credit the prior instrumentation and prediction explicitly. Preregister an accepted-only ablation with the same forecast horizon, guard cohort, and model flexibility.
    69	- [medium] The timing intervention does not place seeds in the predicted regimes (docs/idea-summaries/06-who-self-improvement-leaves-behind.md:221)
    70	  Training a seed on tail participants can approach the tail optimum. It gives no reason to expect the seed to lie beyond that optimum, which is the proposed inverted-U condition. The default seed's position is also unknown. Classifying lineages by their observed shape would describe trajectories without testing whether initial geometry predicts them.
    71	  Recommendation: Assign regimes before each run using an initial local edit panel and an independently defined perturbation direction. Test the geometry in a parameterized harness with measurable optima, and report the open-ended timing analysis separately.
    72	- [medium] The guarantee figure compares different failure events (docs/idea-summaries/images/06/guarantee-validity-and-price.svg:20-30)
    73	  The axis labels every row as the probability of ever crossing the margin. The proposal limits certification to the deployed checkpoint, which can pass even when the underlying search previously crossed the margin. The comparison implies trajectory-wide protection from certification and leaves the simulated noise regime unstated despite the draft's skew warning.
    74	  Recommendation: Plot the same event across methods or label deployed-checkpoint risk separately from any-round risk. State the simulated noise law and qualify the guarantee by per-test validity.
    75	
    76	Next steps:
    77	- Correct the drift estimands and preregister a forecast comparison that isolates the information in rejected candidates.
    78	- Separate certification from audit and verify cohort counts, gate power, and evaluation costs before launching the full study.

