/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/idea-summaries/06-who-self-improvement-leaves-behind.md' in /Users/theodoremui/dev/bioharness
 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Who does self-improvement leave behind?
     2	
     3	> **Research question.** When a self-improving agent keeps only edits that lower its average error, how does a small group fixed in advance (people at risk of falling) fare, and can the loop forecast this early from the candidates it scores?
     4	>
     5	> **Why it matters.** Balance tools exist to find people who balance poorly, about one in seven in StaBLE, yet GEPA, Meta-Harness and the Darwin Gödel Machine select edits on scores the majority dominates. No published study tracks a subgroup's held-out error across such a loop's generations.
     6	>
     7	> **ML contribution.** A held-out estimate of the covariance between an edit's effect on the group and the score that selects it, which sets the group's fate, with a forecast and a dose-response test built on it and a sample-size rule for protective gates.
     8	>
     9	> **What would prove it wrong.** A generation-10 forecast of the generation-50 change no better than extrapolating the group's own curve, or Global-MMLU tail drift that does not change sign where that covariance crosses zero.
    10	
    11	## The problem, in one concrete case
    12	
    13	In the StaBLE "rise to toes" task a participant rises onto the toes, holds and comes down while phones record it. The task score averages features such as peak upward center-of-mass speed and peak ankle angle, each rescaled to 0 to 1 between cohort bounds. StaBLE covers 180 people aged 18 to 87 on 18 tasks, from elite balancers to people at risk of falling [1](#ref-1), [2](#ref-2). Consider an agent built on Qwen3-VL-8B, an 8-billion-parameter vision-language model [3](#ref-3), that receives one phone's video (Cam0), calls RTMPose for 2D keypoints [4](#ref-4) or OpenCap Monocular for 3D kinematics [5](#ref-5), computes the features in code and returns a score. A reflective optimizer such as GEPA [6](#ref-6) rewrites the agent's prompts, tool routes and code, keeping each rewrite that lowers mean absolute error on development people (those the loop scores while it edits).
    14	
    15	Most development people are healthy adults who rise steadily, and edits that help them can hurt the rest. A stronger low-pass filter tuned to quiet standing may flatten the large excursions that define poor balance. A clamp toward typical feature values biases the extremes clinicians care about most, and the faster RTMPose route may lose track during a protective step (a step taken to stop a fall). These are hypotheses; a five-person perturbed-walking study of older adults found more missed gait events, though not significantly [7](#ref-7). If people at fall risk are one in eight, the acceptance test (the gate) passes an edit that raises each of their errors by 0.07 task-score units whenever it lowers everyone else's by just over 0.01. After 50 generations (rounds of propose, score and keep), the mean can have fallen at every step while their error climbed (Figure 1). We call such a group, fixed before the loop starts, the tail. A lab pilot (internal account) where fine-tuning raised error at the extremes points to a second cause, shrinkage, which H4 separates from selection.
    16	
    17	![Under the mean rule the mean error falls at every generation while the tail error first falls and then rises past its starting value.](images/06/concept-mean-vs-tail.svg)
    18	
    19	*Figure 1.* Illustrative trajectories under the mean gate: mean error (blue) falls throughout, while fall-risk-group error (orange) bottoms out at the first marker and passes its starting value at the second.
    20	
    21	The same structure appears in a tool-using question-answering agent on Global-MMLU, which poses MMLU questions in 42 languages [8](#ref-8). If the development set mirrors usage, high-resource languages (those with abundant web text) dominate it. An edit that routes every question through an English translation tool may then raise average accuracy while lowering it in languages the translator handles poorly, again a hypothesis.
    22	
    23	The question is whether a loop can predict how its tail will fare on sealed test people from candidates it already scores on a held-out guard set, and which acceptance rule protects the tail most cheaply. It is open because published work tracks items or averages (next section).
    24	
    25	## Key ideas in plain terms
    26	
    27	**Harness, loop and child.** The harness is everything around the base model: prompts, tool choice and order, tool parameters and the code that combines outputs. In a self-improvement loop a proposer model edits the current harness (the parent); each edited copy is a child, or candidate, and the loop keeps some as new parents.
    28	
    29	**Gate.** The gate is the test a candidate must pass to be kept. The mean gate keeps a child whose average development score beats its parent's; best-of-$K$ keeps the best of $K$ candidates if it passes. The Darwin Gödel Machine (DGM) instead samples parents from an archive of all candidates [9](#ref-9).
    30	
    31	**Tail.** Here a tail is a group of people fixed before the loop starts, by recorded covariates (the fall-risk group, age 70 and above) or by outcome (the bottom score decile). Outcome-defined tails suffer regression to the mean (people picked for an extreme score move toward average when remeasured, whatever the edit), so the primary tail uses covariates.
    32	
    33	**People sets.** The loop selects on development people. Guard people score every candidate but stay hidden from the proposer and the gate (in StaBLE they also certify frozen checkpoints), and sealed test people evaluate finished checkpoints only.
    34	
    35	**Guard log, panel and drift.** The guard log records each candidate's guard-measured tail effect next to the gate's decision. A panel is a batch of single-step edits of one parent, all scored. Tail drift is the expected tail gain one round of selection produces.
    36	
    37	**Correlated-response law.** When selection acts on one trait, a correlated trait moves by its regression on the selected trait times the shift selection produced [10](#ref-10), [11](#ref-11). Here the selected trait is the gate's score $S$ and the correlated trait is an edit's true tail gain $b$. The tail's expected change per accepted edit is then its average edit effect plus a term proportional to $C$, the covariance of $b$ with $S$. When $C$ is negative, the harder the gate selects, the more the tail loses (Figure 2). Price's equation writes the same response as a covariance between trait and selection weight, with no Gaussian model, and so also covers DGM-style parent sampling [11](#ref-11).
    38	
    39	**Winner's curse and shrinkage.** The best of several noisy scores overstates its value, having been picked partly for its noise, which inflates reported gains in LLM self-improvement loops [13](#ref-13), [14](#ref-14). Shrinkage, pulling predictions toward the mean, lowers average error without selection and raises it for people far from the mean.
    40	
    41	**Non-inferiority and bio-creep.** A non-inferiority test asks whether a new version is at most a margin $M$ worse than a reference. Comparing each version only with its predecessor lets permitted losses accumulate (bio-creep, a drug-development term Feng and colleagues applied to medical-device software [16](#ref-16)), which comparing every candidate with the seed $h_0$ prevents.
    42	
    43	**Alpha-spending and fixed-sequence testing.** Alpha-spending splits one error budget $\alpha$ over many tests [17](#ref-17); fixed-sequence testing tests hypotheses in a preset order at full $\alpha$ and stops at the first failure, controlling false rejections under any dependence.
    44	
    45	![Edits accepted by the mean rule include many that harm the tail, because the acceptance line is nearly vertical when the tail is one eighth of the score.](images/06/edit-effect-scatter.svg)
    46	
    47	*Figure 2.* Illustrative edits by estimated majority and tail gain, drawn with a correlation of $-0.4$. At tail weight 0.125 the acceptance line is nearly vertical, so many accepted edits harm the tail (orange).
    48	
    49	## What is known and what is missing
    50	
    51	Agent studies show aggregate gains hiding item and capability losses [18](#ref-18), [19](#ref-19), [20](#ref-20) and keep-if-better acting as selection under noise [14](#ref-14); elsewhere, mean-driven training widens disparities [21](#ref-21), [22](#ref-22), [23](#ref-23) and hides clinical failures on rare subsets [24](#ref-24). Tail-protective selection [25](#ref-25), [26](#ref-26), [27](#ref-27), [16](#ref-16) and the correlated response [12](#ref-12), [10](#ref-10), [11](#ref-11) are established, so no new formula is claimed.
    52	
    53	| Work | What it showed | What it leaves open for this question |
    54	|---|---|---|
    55	| Mitra and Mahmud 2026 [18](#ref-18) | Item-level churn exceeded the net change at least fivefold in five of nine configurations | Measures items; no human subgroups, rule or forecast |
    56	| SAGE 2026 [19](#ref-19) | A per-item paired test cut regressions in 19 of 20 settings | A small group can lose on every edit while wins elsewhere pass the test |
    57	| Hu and Tang 2026 [14](#ref-14) | Keep-if-better is selection under noise; fresh rescoring removes the mean bias; instrumented runs score every candidate on held-out items | Only the mean; no joint distribution of gains across groups, no long-horizon forecast |
    58	| Wei and colleagues 2026 [28](#ref-28) | Retention bounds and fresh-data admission for harness evolution | No subgroup guarantee stated |
    59	| CVaR-RMD 2026 [25](#ref-25) | Selection on CVaR (conditional value at risk, the mean loss of the worst fraction) in LLM-driven program evolution helps tails | Tails over test conditions; no groups of people, mechanism or guarantee |
    60	| Cooper and Lenski 2000; Blanquart 2014 [29](#ref-29), [30](#ref-30) | Early trade-offs in evolving *E. coli*; conflicting effects near an optimum in Fisher's model | Which timing holds for agent edits |
    61	
    62	No study we found tracks a pre-defined human subgroup's sealed-test error across an accept-if-better agent loop's generations, forecasts it from the loop's records, or sizes the tail sample a protective gate needs. Hu and Tang score every candidate on held-out items and model each generation's winner's inflation [14](#ref-14). The contribution therefore rests on the covariance $C$ between an edit's tail effect and the score that selects it, which a guard set estimates from candidates the loop already produces and the law turns into a drift forecast and a prediction of which rules fail.
    63	
    64	## Hypotheses
    65	
    66	Two tests carry the proposal: H2 in E4's rescoring arm, where frozen edits are re-selected at four tail weights so only selection changes, and H1 in E3, where a forecast frozen at generation 10 must beat extrapolation at generation 50. The table at the top of Experiments maps each hypothesis to its test.
    67	
    68	**H1, forecast.** A forecast of the sealed-test change in tail error from generation 0 to 50, fitted to the guard log of generations 0 to 10 and frozen there, beats two comparators in paired absolute error across loops: linear extrapolation of the tail's guard-measured curve, and the same forecast fitted on accepted candidates alone. Its 90% interval covers the realized change in at least 80% of loops. Global-MMLU is the primary test and the StaBLE fall-risk group a registered secondary.
    69	
    70	**H2, dose-response.** In Global-MMLU, raising the tail weight $\pi$ moves the selection term (defined in Method) from negative to positive within one grid step of the $\pi$ at which $C$, estimated on held-out edits, crosses zero. Changing the number of tail items scored, $n_B$, rescales the term without flipping its sign, first on frozen candidates and then in full loops.
    71	
    72	**H3, timing.** Two rivals are registered: drift is negative from generation 0, like the early trade-offs Cooper and Lenski found in *E. coli* [29](#ref-29) (H3a), or positive and then negative, as Fisher's geometric model (each group's loss grows with distance from its own optimum; Method) predicts for a seed beyond the tail optimum [30](#ref-30) (H3b). The sign of drift predicted before a run must match the sign in its later panels.
    73	
    74	**H4, mechanism.** If the mean gate harms the StaBLE tail, part of that harm survives a shrinkage-neutral criterion, error after a per-fold affine recalibration fitted on development people.
    75	
    76	**H5, price and validity.** (a) The cumulative per-group gate with fresh gate items breaches the margin $M$ in at most 5% of loops. (b) At each $n_B$ it keeps a share of majority gain within the interval from rerunning A4 with E1's moments. (c) When $C<0$, the rules order by tail drift as Baselines predicts.
    77	
    78	## Method
    79	
    80	The law says when selection on the mean harms the tail, and the timing model when that sign turns. The guard log and forecast measure the law's inputs in a running loop, and the gate, sample-size rule and validity conditions decide which rule protects the tail most cheaply. Preliminary simulations in this project ([`sim/`](../../output/idea-summaries-build/sim/)) check each part: A1 the closed form, A2 best-of-$K$, A3 the geometric model, guard estimator and forecast, and A4 the protective gates.
    81	
    82	### Notation and the correlated-response law
    83	
    84	Harnesses are $h$, with seed $h_0$, and generations are $t$. A candidate edit's effects are its true reductions in mean loss relative to its parent, $a$ on the majority and $b$ on the tail, jointly Gaussian over the proposer's edits with means $\mu_a,\mu_b$, spreads $\sigma_a,\sigma_b$ and correlation $\rho$. Gains are positive when error falls; harms are positive when tail error rises. The tail's weight in the objective, $\pi$, defaults to its population share (about 0.14 for StaBLE's fall-risk group) and is set by design in Global-MMLU.
    85	
    86	The numbers of people scored, $n_A$ and $n_B$, are kept separate from $\pi$, so oversampling tail people cuts noise without changing the objective. With per-person variance $v$ of the child-minus-parent loss, the loop's estimates $\hat a$ and $\hat b$ carry independent noise of variance $s_a^2=v/n_A$ and $s_b^2=v/n_B$. The gate accepts when the score $S=(1-\pi)\hat a+\pi\hat b$ exceeds a threshold $\tau$ ($\tau=0$ for the plain mean gate).
    87	
    88	Because $(b,S)$ is bivariate Gaussian with $C=\mathrm{Cov}(b,S)$, regressing $b$ on $S$ gives, for any selection that depends on $S$ alone,
    89	
    90	$$\mathbb{E}[b\mid\text{selected}]=\mu_b+\frac{C}{\sigma_S^2}\Big(\mathbb{E}[S\mid\text{selected}]-\mu_S\Big),\qquad C=(1-\pi)\rho\sigma_a\sigma_b+\pi\sigma_b^2,$$
    91	
    92	$$\mu_S=(1-\pi)\mu_a+\pi\mu_b,\qquad \sigma_S^2=(1-\pi)^2(\sigma_a^2+s_a^2)+\pi^2(\sigma_b^2+s_b^2)+2\pi(1-\pi)\rho\sigma_a\sigma_b .$$
    93	
    94	For the plain mean gate, with $z=\mu_S/\sigma_S$ and the inverse Mills ratio $\lambda(z)=\phi(z)/\Phi(z)$ (normal density over distribution function, Heckman's selection correction [12](#ref-12)),
    95	
    96	$$\mathbb{E}[b\mid S>0]=\mu_b+\frac{C}{\sigma_S}\,\lambda(z),\qquad D=\mathbb{E}\big[b\,\mathbf{1}\{S>0\}\big]=\mu_b\,\Phi(z)+\frac{C}{\sigma_S}\,\phi(z).$$
    97	
    98	The drift per proposal $D$, the expected tail gain from one candidate facing the gate alone, is the tail's average edit effect times the acceptance rate plus a selection term $(C/\sigma_S)\phi(z)$. The selection term is the covariance of an edit's tail effect with the gate's decision, $\mathrm{Cov}(b,\mathbf{1}\{S>0\})$, which the guard log estimates directly. Its sign is that of $C$, and noise enters it only through $\sigma_S$, so more tail evaluation rescales the selection term but cannot flip it. Total $D$ can still flip with noise when $\mu_b$ and $C$ differ in sign, because noise also moves the acceptance rate.
    99	
   100	With $\mu_b\le0$ the tail loses on every accepted edit in expectation whenever $\rho$ is below the correlation at which $C=0$,
   101	
   102	$$\rho^*=-\frac{\pi\,\sigma_b}{(1-\pi)\,\sigma_a}.$$
   103	
   104	At $\pi=0.125$, $\rho^*$ is $-0.143$ for equal spreads and $-0.571$ when the tail's spread is four times larger (Figure 3). A1 confirmed the closed form in 1,344 cells, 255 of which drifted negative outside this sufficient condition. Best-of-$K$ replaces $\lambda(z)$ with $m_K(c)$, the mean of the largest of $K$ standard normals given that it exceeds $c=(\tau-\mu_S)/\sigma_S$, which grows roughly like $\sqrt{2\ln K}$ and amplifies the selection term in the direction of $C$ (5.6-fold from $K=1$ to 50 in A2).
   105	
   106	![The tail loses under selection below a threshold correlation that depends on the tail's share of the objective and its relative spread.](images/06/drift-sign-map.svg)
   107	
   108	*Figure 3.* Computed from the law: the threshold correlation $\rho^*$ against the tail's share $\pi$ for three ratios of tail to majority spread. Below a curve the selection term harms the tail; at one tail person in eight (vertical line), once $\rho<-0.143$ for equal spreads.
   109	
   110	### When the sign changes
   111	
   112	In Fisher's geometric model from evolutionary biology [30](#ref-30), the harness is a point $x$, each group's loss is the squared distance to its optimum ($x_A$ or $x_B$), and an edit is a random step with covariance $\Sigma_\delta$. With the mean optimum at $x_w=(1-\pi)x_A+\pi x_B$, to leading order in step size
   113	
   114	$$C\approx 4\,(x-x_B)^\top\Sigma_\delta\,(x-x_w),$$
   115	
   116	which is negative when the tail and mean gradients conflict once weighted by the covariance of proposed steps. For isotropic edits this happens, to the same order, inside the ball whose diameter joins $x_B$ and $x_w$. The seed's position relative to the ball sets one of three regimes (Figure 4).
   117	
   118	A3 reproduced the three regimes in ten dimensions. Its inverted U peaked while $\rho$ was still above $\rho^*$, because random steps cost the tail on average ($\mu_b<0$), so the turn comes where $D$ changes sign, slightly before ball entry, and E5 predicts that sign.
   119	
   120	![Three regimes of tail trajectory, set by where the seed harness sits relative to the ball that joins the tail optimum and the mean optimum.](images/06/timing-regimes-geometry.svg)
   121	
   122	*Figure 4.* Schematic. Inside the shaded ball the expected tail gain per generation is negative to leading order in step size (the exact turn comes slightly earlier). Seed 1 stays outside and the tail gains; seed 2 enters at the diamond, where its cumulative tail gain (right) peaks before falling, possibly below zero; seed 3 declines from the start.
   123	
   124	### Estimating drift from the guard log
   125	
   126	Each candidate is scored on development people, giving the gate's input $S_{\text{dev}}$, and on disjoint guard people, giving its tail effect $\hat b_{\text{guard}}$ relative to its parent. In a panel (E1) each of $m$ edits of one parent faces the gate alone, and
   127	
   128	$$\hat D=\frac{1}{m}\sum_{e=1}^{m}\hat b_{\text{guard},e}\,\mathbf{1}\{S_{\text{dev},e}>\tau\},\qquad \hat D_{\text{sel}}=\hat D-\hat\mu_b\,\hat P,$$
   129	
   130	where $\hat\mu_b$ and $\hat P$ are the panel's mean guard effect and acceptance rate. In a running loop the incumbent's change $\hat\Delta_t$ in generation $t$ is the kept child's guard effect. These increments are differences on the same guard people, so along the lineage they sum to the guard-measured change since $h_0$, which checks the log; in a DGM-style archive the sum follows the reported best harness's lineage.
   131	
   132	Guard noise is independent of development noise, so both estimates, and the covariance of $\hat b_{\text{guard}}$ with $S_{\text{dev}}$ over all candidates as an estimate of $C$, are unbiased without a Gaussian assumption. The naive correlation of $\hat a$ and $\hat b$ is attenuated by noise (in A3 a true 0.6 read as 0.21 when $s_b=2\sigma_b$, while $\hat D$ stayed within 0.004 of the truth), so it is reported both raw and disattenuated (corrected for noise using bootstrap noise variances).
   133	
   134	### The forecast
   135	
   136	At generation 10 a forecast of the tail change from generation 0 to 50 is frozen and registered. It is a mean-field recursion, replacing each generation's random outcome by its expectation: each generation adds the kept child's expected majority gain and tail change, $\mathbb{E}[b_{(K)}\mathbf{1}\{S_{(K)}>\tau\}]$ for the top-scoring candidate under best-of-$K$. The moments $\mu_b$, $\sigma_b$ and $C$ are linear in the cumulative majority gain $\Gamma_t$, with an exponential approach to a limit as the registered alternative.
   137	
   138	A loop without its own panel logs only 44 candidates by generation 10 for six coefficients, so moments are fitted hierarchically across seeds of the same condition, with the matching generation-0 panel, where one exists, as the prior for the intercepts. Intervals combine a bootstrap over guard people and candidates with seed-to-seed spread. With known moment functions the recursion called every inverted U correctly in A3 (20 of 20 cells); H1 tests whether moments fitted over ten generations extrapolate to fifty.
   139	
   140	### A design rule for tail sample size
   141	
   142	Take a candidate with cumulative tail harm $R$ relative to $h_0$ and per-person harm spread $\sigma_R$. A one-sided test of $R\ge M$ at level $\alpha_l$ passes it with probability about
   143	
   144	$$\Phi\Big(\frac{(M-R)\sqrt{n_B}}{\sigma_R}-\Phi^{-1}(1-\alpha_l)\Big),$$
   145	
   146	with $\Phi^{-1}$ the standard normal quantile. Requiring pass probability $p^\star$ at $\bar R$, the typical cumulative harm of proposed candidates, with $\alpha$ split evenly over $L$ tests, gives
   147	
   148	$$n_B\ \ge\ \frac{\sigma_R^2\,\big(\Phi^{-1}(1-\alpha/L)+\Phi^{-1}(p^\star)\big)^2}{(M-\bar R)^2}.$$
   149	
   150	With $\alpha=0.05$, $L=50$ and $p^\star=0.8$ the squared term is $(3.09+0.84)^2=15.4$. In A4 per-person harm is a random walk with per-edit spread 0.25, so after 25 accepted edits $\sigma_R=1.25$, and with $\bar R=0$ and $M=0.5$ the rule asks for about 97 tail people per test. The rule sets the scale of $n_B$; because the share of majority gain kept also depends on how $\sigma_R$ grows along the lineage, H5(b) predicts it by simulation.
   151	
   152	### Valid protective designs
   153	
   154	The protective gate accepts a candidate only if its estimated majority gain is positive and a one-sided test at level $\alpha_l$ rejects a cumulative tail harm since $h_0$ of at least $M$. Suppose each test is valid given everything that produced the candidate (V1, true when its tail people are independent of the loop's history) and $\sum_l\alpha_l\le\alpha$ over all tests (V2). A union bound then shows that no accepted parent is ever more than $M$ worse than $h_0$ on the tail, except with probability at most $\alpha$, the group-wise analogue of the bounds of Wei and colleagues [28](#ref-28) and Feng and colleagues [16](#ref-16).
   155	
   156	In A4 (20,000 loops per cell; Figure 5), reusing the same tail people every round gave breach rates of 0.11 to 0.29 at nominal 0.05. A parent accepted on a lucky sample passes its bias to every descendant, the holdout-reuse problem of adaptive data analysis [31](#ref-31). Per-step non-inferiority against the parent breached in every loop. Two designs stayed valid under normal and heavy-tailed symmetric noise: fresh tail people for every test (at most 0.0013), which needs a large tail and tells the proposer only whether each candidate passed, and checkpoint certification (0.016 to 0.019). Certification tests frozen checkpoints in generation order, by fixed-sequence testing, on tail people the loop never used, and covers only the deployed harness. Right-skewed harm breaks the test itself: lognormal harm took the $t$ test to 0.17 to 0.23, and a harm confined to 10% of tail people escapes any test with probability $0.9^{n_B}$.
   157	
   158	![Reusing the same tail people breaks the cumulative guarantee, while fresh people and sealed checkpoint certification keep it; the share of majority gain kept rises with the number of tail people.](images/06/guarantee-validity-and-price.svg)
   159	
   160	*Figure 5.* Simulation A4, normal per-person noise. Left: with edits pushing against the margin, how often an accepted harness is more than $M$ worse than the seed on the tail (any accepted parent for gates, the deployed checkpoint for certification). Bars span 5 to 20 tail people per test. Right: majority gain kept under tail-neutral edits.
   161	
   162	### The instrumented loop
   163	
   164	Figure 6 and the pseudocode show one instrumented run.
   165	
   166	```text
   167	input: seed harness h0, proposer P, people sets dev, guard and test,
   168	       acceptance rule, candidates per generation K, generations 50, tail weight pi
   169	parent <- h0
   170	for t in 0..49:
   171	    cands <- [P.edit(parent, traces_from(dev)) for k in 1..K]   # P never sees guard or test
   172	    for e in cands:
   173	        S_dev[e]   <- score(e, dev, weights = (1 - pi, pi))     # what the rule acts on
   174	        b_guard[e] <- tail_gain(e, parent, guard)               # logged, never shown to P or the rule
   175	    kept <- rule(cands, S_dev)                                  # mean gate, best-of-K, Pareto, ...
   176	    append (t, S_dev, b_guard, kept) to the guard log           # Delta_t = b_guard[kept]
   177	    if kept: parent <- kept; save checkpoint h_t
   178	    if t == 10: freeze and register the forecast of the tail change on test at t = 50
   179	certify the frozen checkpoints on guard by fixed-sequence testing (StaBLE)
   180	                                                                # guard never fed the loop, so this is valid
   181	evaluate the forecast and the deployed checkpoint on test
   182	```
   183	
   184	![The instrumented loop scores every candidate on guard people hidden from the loop, logs the tail effect against the gate decision, and compares a frozen forecast with the change on sealed test people.](images/06/instrumented-loop.svg)
   185	
   186	*Figure 6.* Blue boxes are the ordinary loop. The grey guard set scores every candidate but never feeds the loop (in StaBLE it also certifies checkpoints); the green sealed test set scores saved checkpoints only after the run; orange marks the guard log and frozen forecast.
   187	
   188	## Experiments
   189	
   190	| Hypothesis | Tested in | Domain | Fails if |
   191	|---|---|---|---|
   192	| H1 forecast | E3, using E1 panels and E2 loops | Global-MMLU primary, StaBLE secondary | error not below both comparators', or coverage under 80% |
   193	| H2 dose-response | E4 rescoring arm, then loop arm | Global-MMLU | selection term's sign wrong in more than two of 12 cells, or $n_B$ flips it |
   194	| H3 timing | E5 | StaBLE parameterized harness; open-ended lineages | predicted sign of $D$ wrong in more than 2 of 10 lineages |
   195	| H4 mechanism | E7 | StaBLE | tail harm vanishes after affine recalibration |
   196	| H5 price and validity | E6 | Global-MMLU | breach rate significantly above 5%, or gain kept outside its simulated interval |
   197	
   198	### Protocol
   199	
   200	People (or questions) are split before any panel, cache or library is built, and sealed test people are never touched by an optimizer. Cross-fitting (rotating which block plays each role, so every person is tested once) handles small subgroups. Every loop runs at least five seeds, since single self-improvement runs mislead [32](#ref-32), and intervals are person-clustered bootstraps.
   201	
   202	### Data and splits
   203	
   204	**StaBLE.** The lab pilot (internal account) counted 171 participants with 2,968 usable Cam0 task examples, and fall covariates are in the public release [2](#ref-2). The harness covers five tasks fixed in advance, including rise to toes but not Y-Balance, whose reference reach-window method failed on single-camera video in the pilot. Five blocks stratified by fall-risk group and age rotate as sealed test (about 34 people), guard (about 34) and development (about 103) (Figure 7). Certification uses the guard block and the test block measures the deployed checkpoint, so no person both certifies and measures a harness. Feature bounds come from each fold's development people; test scores outside 0 to 1 stay unclipped. The 98 trials skipped for safety (scored 0 in the release) and 3 imputed trials [1](#ref-1) form a separate stratum, so no agent gains by learning the assignment rule.
   205	
   206	The primary tail is the recorded fall-risk group (4 or more on the 12-item CDC Stay Independent questionnaire), with age 70 and above and diagnosis as secondary tails. The StaBLE paper counts 25 of 180 participants at risk [1](#ref-1), about five per block ($\pi\approx0.14$), and we confirm the usable counts before registration. A person's loss is the mean absolute task-score error over that person's tasks.
   207	
   208	**Global-MMLU.** Each language has about 14,000 test questions [8](#ref-8). Items are split by question identifier, so all 42 translations of a question share a pool: about 2,000 identifiers for development, 1,000 for guard, 6,000 for fresh gate items and 5,000 for sealed test. Majority and tail items in a set use disjoint identifiers, so their noise is independent as the law assumes, and no run reuses a gate identifier. Panels score each edit on 512 majority and 512 tail development items, which E4 resamples. The tail is the 13 languages the Global-MMLU paper classes as low-resource [8](#ref-8).
   209	
   210	![Five outer folds rotate test, guard and development blocks of StaBLE participants so that every person is sealed test once.](images/06/stable-person-splits.svg)
   211	
   212	*Figure 7.* Person-level cross-fitting for StaBLE: in each outer fold one block is sealed test, the next is the guard set and the other three are development people.
   213	
   214	### Models and tools
   215	
   216	The StaBLE agent runs Qwen3-VL-8B [3](#ref-3) with OpenCap Monocular [5](#ref-5), RTMPose [4](#ref-4), center of mass from OpenSim musculoskeletal models [33](#ref-33), a configurable filter and the StaBLE feature code. The proposer, Qwen3-Coder-480B-A35B-Instruct (a 480-billion-parameter mixture-of-experts coding model with 35 billion active) [34](#ref-34), writes $K=4$ candidates per generation. It drives three loop families: (L1) a GEPA-style optimizer of prompts and tool routes [6](#ref-6); (L2) a Meta-Harness-style optimizer of harness code that sees earlier candidates' code and traces [35](#ref-35); and (L3) a DGM-style archive [9](#ref-9). The Global-MMLU agent runs the same base model on text, with a translation tool backed by that model, under L1 and L2.
   217	
   218	### Baselines
   219	
   220	All rules share seeds and seed harnesses within each loop family.
   221	
   222	1. **Mean gate**, GEPA's acceptance step alone [6](#ref-6).
   223	2. **GEPA Pareto**, which also samples parents among candidates that lead on some instance, weighted by how many they lead [6](#ref-6).
   224	3. **SAGE**, committing a child only if a paired test across items favors it [19](#ref-19).
   225	4. **CVaR over people**, ranking candidates by the worst 20% of development people [25](#ref-25).
   226	5. **GroupDRO weighting** (group distributionally robust optimization) of the two groups by exponentiated running loss [15](#ref-15), [26](#ref-26).
   227	6. **Risk-bound selection**, requiring a high-probability tail-loss bound no worse than the parent's [27](#ref-27).
   228	7. **aACP-BAC**, Feng and colleagues' automatic algorithmic change protocol, whose alpha-investing, group-sequential and gatekeeping tests bound the bad approval count, here on tail loss [16](#ref-16).
   229	8. **Fresh-holdout rescoring** of the kept candidate on never-selected items [14](#ref-14).
   230	9. **Cumulative per-group gate**, with fresh gate items in Global-MMLU and checkpoint certification of the mean-gate L1 lineages in StaBLE.
   231	
   232	When $C<0$ and $n_B$ is small, the law predicts an order. Rules 1 and 8 drift most, since rescoring removes the mean's optimism and leaves $C$ unchanged, and rule 3 drifts less because it accepts less. Rules 4 and 5 raise the tail's weight and turn drift positive past the crossing point without a guarantee, and only rules 7 and 9 bound cumulative harm.
   233	
   234	### Metrics
   235	
   236	The primary metric is the change in sealed-test tail error from generation 0 to 50: person-level mean absolute task-score error for the fall-risk group, error rate for the tail languages. Secondary metrics are drift and its selection term, forecast error and coverage, majority gain kept, margin breaches, per-group bias, calibration slope (reference regressed on predicted score) and tokens.
   237	
   238	### Core experiments
   239	
   240	- **E1, panels.** At generations 0, 10, 25 and 50 of one mean-gate L1 lineage per StaBLE fold and of five Global-MMLU lineages, score 200 single-step edits of the parent on development and guard people to estimate $\hat D$, $\hat D_{\text{sel}}$ and $C$.
   241	- **E2, loops.** In StaBLE, run the mean gate in L1 to L3 in five folds (75 loops) and rules 2 to 6 in L1 in two folds (50 loops); rule 9 certifies the mean-gate L1 checkpoints. In Global-MMLU, run all nine rules in L1 and L2 at $\pi=0.1$ and $n_B=64$ (90 loops).
   242	- **E3, forecast (H1).** For every mean-gate loop, compare the frozen forecast at generations 25 and 50 with H1's two comparators and with two secondary ones (tail change proportional to mean change; no change). StaBLE's H1 stays confirmatory only if its generation-0 panel predicts a standard error for a loop's realized change below the smallest effect of interest; otherwise the age-70-and-above tail is checked the same way, and failing that StaBLE's H1 is descriptive.
   243	- **E4, dose-response (H2).** In Global-MMLU, cross $\pi\in\{0.05,0.1,0.2,0.4\}$ with $n_B\in\{16,64,256\}$ (Figure 8). The rescoring arm reapplies the gate to frozen E1 edits with resampled development items, so only selection varies; moments come from a random half of the edits and the selection term is measured on the other half. The loop arm runs the mean gate in L1 with five seeds per cell, 256 majority items and proposer traces from one fixed 64-item minibatch (60 loops). If the fitting half of the generation-0 panel gives $\hat\rho\ge0$, the tail is first narrowed to the languages where forced English translation does worst; a predicted crossing outside the grid shifts the grid.
   244	- **E5, timing (H3).** A parameterized StaBLE harness, with random-step edits to filter cutoff, clamp strength and tool-route weight (the share of clips sent to RTMPose), runs on cached tool outputs. Grid search on development people locates both optima, so seeds are placed in each regime by construction, though random steps stand in for LLM proposals. In the ten open-ended panel lineages, the first two panels predict the sign of $D$ at generation 50.
   245	- **E6, price and validity (H5).** In Global-MMLU, run rule 9 and its reused-item variant at $n_B\in\{16,48,112\}$ in L1, ten seeds each (60 loops), with the forced-translation edit offered to the proposer every generation so that candidates press against the margin. A breach is called when an accepted parent's error on 1,000 sealed tail items exceeds the seed's by more than $M$. Breach rates and gain kept are compared with nominal and with A4 rerun on E1's per-item harms.
   246	- **E7, mechanism (H4).** Tag accepted edits that narrow the spread of predictions (clamps, heavier filters), and recompute the primary contrast after per-fold affine recalibration and as within-group rank correlation.
   247	
   248	![A four-by-three grid of tail share and tail evaluation size in which the law predicts harm for tail shares up to 0.2 and gain at 0.4.](images/06/dose-response-design.svg)
   249	
   250	*Figure 8.* Illustrative E4 prediction from the law: correlation $-0.3$, equal spreads, zero mean effects (so the selection term is all of the drift), one proposal, 256 majority items and per-item noise variance 64 in squared majority-spread units. The sign flips near $\pi=0.23$, and more tail items change only the size; registered predictions use held-out E1 moments.
   251	
   252	### Ablations and controls
   253	
   254	- **Pseudo-tail and permuted labels.** A random majority group of the tail's size, or shuffled tail membership, should show no differential drift, which calibrates noise and estimator bias.
   255	- **Selection strength.** L1 with $K=1$ and $K=8$ should rescale the selection term by $m_K(c)/\lambda(z)$ without flipping its sign.
   256	- **Trace composition.** Proposer traces (the logged inputs, tool calls and outputs shown to the proposer) drawn half from tail languages, against random traces, separate selection from what the proposer sees. Both run in Global-MMLU with five seeds per arm.
   257	
   258	### Statistical analysis
   259	
   260	Outcomes are tested in a registered fixed sequence at $\alpha=0.05$, stopping at the first failure. The order is H2's sign agreement in the rescoring arm, H1 in Global-MMLU, H1 in StaBLE if E3's power check passes, and last the StaBLE mean gate's sealed-test change in fall-risk error. H2 passes with the sign right in at least 10 of 12 cells (probability 0.02 under random signs), and the slope of observed on predicted selection terms is reported. With no test-retest data for a minimal detectable change, the smallest effect of interest is 0.03 task-score units, a quarter of the StaBLE score's between-person standard deviation of 0.12 [1](#ref-1), re-expressed in development people's per-person error spread before registration. The margin $M$ equals it (three points of error rate in Global-MMLU), with 0.02 and 0.05 as sensitivity checks.
   261	
   262	H1 uses paired absolute forecast errors in a mixed model with random intercepts for fold and seed, since loops in a fold share test people; loops accepting fewer than five edits, where every forecast is near zero, are reported separately. Trajectories, including the StaBLE primary change, use a mixed model with rule, family and their interaction as fixed effects and seed and fold as random intercepts, and each family is also reported alone. Secondary contrasts are paired through shared seeds and Holm-corrected, and "the tail tracks the mean" is an equivalence test at the smallest effect of interest.
   263	
   264	On power, 200-edit panels gave $\hat D$ a standard deviation of 0.05 to 0.10 $\sigma_a$ in A3, enough to detect drift per proposal of 0.13 to 0.29 $\sigma_a$ at 80%. A StaBLE fold's realized change rests on about five test fall-risk people shared by its 15 loops, so Global-MMLU carries H1.
   265	
   266	A4 found the guarantee credible only from about 20 tail people per test, so StaBLE certification is exploratory. Its paired per-person differences are clipped to $[-1,1]$ and tested with a one-sided Hoeffding bound (finite-sample, for bounded variables), which at five people and $\alpha=0.05$ cannot certify a margin below about 1.1 task-score units even if every difference is zero. Global-MMLU reports breach rates with Clopper-Pearson (exact binomial) intervals. Pooled over three tail sizes, 30 loops per rule reject a 5% rate with probability 0.97 when the true rate is 30%; no feasible count confirms 5%, so that rerun of A4 also checks the nominal level.
   267	
   268	### Second domain
   269	
   270	Global-MMLU, with a tail fixed in advance and sized by design, tests generality for H1, H2 and H5 (limits: multiple-choice format, possible contamination).
   271	
   272	## How to read the results
   273	
   274	| Outcome | What it would mean | What we would do next |
   275	|---|---|---|
   276	| $\hat C\ge0$ in the generation-0 panel | Edits do not trade off for this tail | Move to a conflicting tail |
   277	| The forecast beats extrapolation with calibrated intervals | The guard log is an early warning | Release the instrumented loop; if only StaBLE fails, report the tail size needed |
   278	| Tail change equals mean change in every family | Mean selection is safe for these tails | Report the null; seek stronger trade-offs |
   279	| Drift flips at the predicted $\pi$, or far from it | The law fits real loops, or its moments or Gaussian form mislead | Size development sets with the law, or refit with the Price form |
   280	| Drift is negative from generation 0 (H3a) or turns late (H3b) | Trade-offs arrive early, or after shared gains run out | Gate from the start, or from the measured sign change |
   281	| Tail harm vanishes under shrinkage-neutral criteria | The StaBLE harm is shrinkage | Report a calibration finding; rest the law on Global-MMLU |
   282	| The gate holds its level but keeps under 10% of majority gain at the largest feasible $n_B$ | Protection is priced out at realistic tail sizes | Recommend certification, larger tails |
   283	| The law fits panels but the forecast fails | Moments move unpredictably | Restrict claims to per-generation monitoring |
   284	
   285	## Compute, data and scope
   286	
   287	The arithmetic assumes the 8B model processes 25,000 tokens per second per H100 on this prefill-heavy, prefix-cached load (checked on the first panel).
   288	
   289	- **Tool cache.** 855 StaBLE clips (171 people, five tasks) at about 3.8 minutes of OpenCap Monocular per 17-second clip on one H100 (lab pilot timing) take about 54 H100-hours per configuration, 108 for two. RTMPose-m exceeds 90 frames per second on a CPU [4](#ref-4), so 872,000 frames (at 60 per second) take about 2.7 CPU-hours per variant.
   290	- **StaBLE agent.** About 3,300 tokens per task call gives 16,500 per person, 2.26 million per candidate scored on 137 development and guard people, and 452 million per 200-candidate loop. The 125 loops plus 4,000 panel edits use 65.5 billion tokens and sealed-test scoring of checkpoints 0.2 billion, about 730 H100-hours.
   291	- **Global-MMLU.** At about 1,500 tokens per item, 210 loops of 200 candidates on about 630 items each (256 majority development items, about 120 tail development or gate items on average, 256 guard items) use 39.7 billion tokens. Panels on 1,280 items use 7.7 billion, scoring the accepted parents of 70 protective loops on 1,000 sealed items 2.6 billion, and scoring every loop's generation-25 and 50 checkpoints on 4,000 sealed items 2.5 billion: 52.5 billion tokens, about 583 H100-hours. Fifteen ablation loops add 3.1 billion tokens and 3,250 proposals (58 H100-hours).
   292	- **Proposer.** About 75,000 proposals for the main runs at 33,000 tokens each come to 2.5 billion tokens, about 550 H100-hours at an assumed 10,000 tokens per second per 8-GPU node.
   293	
   294	The total is about 2,030 H100-hours, or 2,540 with a 25% margin for failed runs, on Stanford HAIC, with about 121 billion tokens on the 8B model. StaBLE videos require lab access; the Zenodo release (CC BY-NC 4.0) and Global-MMLU are public.
   295	
   296	## Risks and limitations
   297	
   298	- **Leakage.** An automated check scans every proposer prompt for guard and test identifiers, since any leak voids the guarantee.
   299	- **Labels and data.** Fall-risk status may be noisy and is sensitive, so secondary tails check it and only group aggregates leave the lab's servers.
   300	- **Scope.** Results may be specific to the three loop families and to edits of prompts, routes and code.
   301	
   302	## Where to learn more
   303	
   304	- usaaio.guide, "The Best of Five Noisy Scores" ([link](https://usaaio.guide/theory/?id=skl-best-of-five-noisy-scores)): why the winner's score is biased upward.
   305	- usaaio.guide, "How Often a 95% Interval Covers a Rare Failure Rate" ([link](https://usaaio.guide/theory/?id=si-wald-coverage-for-rare-failures)): why rare tail harms defeat small-sample gates.
   306	- Stanford CS329Z Lecture 9, "Optimization" ([slides](https://web.stanford.edu/class/cs329z/slides/lecture09.pdf)): GEPA, the optimizer the L1 loops follow.
   307	- Stanford CS329A, "Open-Ended Evolution of Self-Improving Agents" ([schedule](https://cs329a.stanford.edu/#schedule)): the Darwin Gödel Machine's archive and parent selection.
