codex
**Verdict** `needs-attention`

Do not run the confirmatory study as written. H2’s significance calculation treats reused evidence as independent, and E6 cannot distinguish a true margin breach from audit sampling error. StaBLE’s certification rule also cannot pass at the planned cohort size.

The revision fixes the best-of-four drift units for a single incumbent, separates the covariance term from total drift, and moves StaBLE certification off the final test cohort. Frozen rescoring addresses the earlier candidate-generation confound in H2’s local arm. The archive forecast, certification feasibility, and protection validation remain open.

1. **High — H2’s first confirmatory test has an invalid null calibration.** `line_start` 260 · `line_end` 260 · confidence 0.99. The 0.02 probability assumes 12 independent fair sign matches. E4 reuses the same held-out edits across cells, and predictions repeat each weight’s sign across all three sample sizes. If the three matches within each weight move together, even four independent fair weight-level matches yield a 6.25% pass rate. H2 is first in the fixed sequence, so its invalid test compromises the confirmatory sequence. **Recommendation** Preregister a joint null test that preserves shared parent batches, question IDs, and the fitting-half tail and grid selection procedure. [Proposal](/Users/theodoremui/dev/bioharness/docs/idea-summaries/06-who-self-improvement-leaves-behind.md:260)

2. **High — E6 measures noisy breach labels and compares them with a population guarantee.** `line_start` 245 · `line_end` 245 · confidence 0.99. An accepted parent just below the true margin can exceed it on 1,000 audit items by chance. Checking every accepted parent can magnify this error, while real breaches near the margin can be missed. The pooled Clopper–Pearson interval also assumes a common breach probability across three gate sizes whose behavior the experiment is designed to vary. **Recommendation** Test error control in a setting with known true harms. For benchmark runs, use simultaneous paired audit intervals, mark unresolved crossings as indeterminate, and report breach estimates by gate size. [Proposal](/Users/theodoremui/dev/bioharness/docs/idea-summaries/06-who-self-improvement-leaves-behind.md:245)

3. **High — The Global-MMLU gate has no specified valid test and may reject even identical checkpoints.** `line_start` 140 · `line_end` 156 · confidence 0.99. Fresh items address adaptive reuse. The guarantee still requires a conditionally valid per-test procedure, which the proposal leaves unspecified. With a 3-point margin and 50 equal spends, a null with harm `+1` on 3% of items and zero elsewhere produces 112 ties with probability 3.3%. A deterministic test at level 0.001 therefore cannot pass an all-tie sample even at E6’s largest size. **Recommendation** Specify the finite-sample paired test and shortlist one candidate before opening each fresh batch. Derive power for that test at realistic rates of changed answers, then resize the sweep or narrow the claim. [Proposal](/Users/theodoremui/dev/bioharness/docs/idea-summaries/06-who-self-improvement-leaves-behind.md:140)

4. **High — StaBLE certification cannot deploy a non-seed checkpoint at the planned count.** `line_start` 266 · `line_end` 266 · confidence 0.99. With five people and differences clipped to `[-1,1]`, even the best possible sample gives a one-sided Hoeffding upper bound of `−1 + 1.095 = 0.095`, above the 0.03 margin. Calling the arm exploratory leaves it unable to measure the price of protection. Clipping also changes the loss being certified relative to the unclipped audit. **Recommendation** Drop the certification arm at the planned count, or recruit enough independent fall-risk participants to demonstrate power for a prespecified bounded endpoint used in both certification and audit. [Proposal](/Users/theodoremui/dev/bioharness/docs/idea-summaries/06-who-self-improvement-leaves-behind.md:266)

5. **High — H1 still lacks a prospective forecast for the archive.** `line_start` 130 · `line_end` 138 · confidence 0.98. The reported best harness’s lineage can be reconstructed after generation 50. At generation 10, the recursion only models the best child of one parent and has no transition for sampling an older parent or replacing the reported incumbent. E3 nevertheless includes every mean-gate loop. **Recommendation** Restrict confirmatory H1 to single-incumbent loops, or specify the archive state, parent-sampling policy, and reporting rule in the forecast and verify it on simulated branch switches. [Proposal](/Users/theodoremui/dev/bioharness/docs/idea-summaries/06-who-self-improvement-leaves-behind.md:130)

6. **High — Prompt scanning leaves a route for guard data to reach future proposals.** `line_start` 169 · `line_end` 177 · confidence 0.94. Editable harness code runs on guard people before the kept child is saved. If that execution writes a cache, trace, or other state reused by the next parent, guard inputs can influence later candidates without any guard identifier appearing in a proposer prompt. The same risk applies across rotating StaBLE folds if they share mutable artifacts. **Recommendation** Execute each split in an isolated environment from an immutable candidate snapshot. Pass only authorized development artifacts to later proposals, and verify at the artifact boundary that guard, test, and cross-fold state cannot enter the optimizer. [Proposal](/Users/theodoremui/dev/bioharness/docs/idea-summaries/06-who-self-improvement-leaves-behind.md:169)
tokens used
81,543
**Verdict** `needs-attention`

Do not run the confirmatory study as written. H2’s significance calculation treats reused evidence as independent, and E6 cannot distinguish a true margin breach from audit sampling error. StaBLE’s certification rule also cannot pass at the planned cohort size.

The revision fixes the best-of-four drift units for a single incumbent, separates the covariance term from total drift, and moves StaBLE certification off the final test cohort. Frozen rescoring addresses the earlier candidate-generation confound in H2’s local arm. The archive forecast, certification feasibility, and protection validation remain open.

1. **High — H2’s first confirmatory test has an invalid null calibration.** `line_start` 260 · `line_end` 260 · confidence 0.99. The 0.02 probability assumes 12 independent fair sign matches. E4 reuses the same held-out edits across cells, and predictions repeat each weight’s sign across all three sample sizes. If the three matches within each weight move together, even four independent fair weight-level matches yield a 6.25% pass rate. H2 is first in the fixed sequence, so its invalid test compromises the confirmatory sequence. **Recommendation** Preregister a joint null test that preserves shared parent batches, question IDs, and the fitting-half tail and grid selection procedure. [Proposal](/Users/theodoremui/dev/bioharness/docs/idea-summaries/06-who-self-improvement-leaves-behind.md:260)

2. **High — E6 measures noisy breach labels and compares them with a population guarantee.** `line_start` 245 · `line_end` 245 · confidence 0.99. An accepted parent just below the true margin can exceed it on 1,000 audit items by chance. Checking every accepted parent can magnify this error, while real breaches near the margin can be missed. The pooled Clopper–Pearson interval also assumes a common breach probability across three gate sizes whose behavior the experiment is designed to vary. **Recommendation** Test error control in a setting with known true harms. For benchmark runs, use simultaneous paired audit intervals, mark unresolved crossings as indeterminate, and report breach estimates by gate size. [Proposal](/Users/theodoremui/dev/bioharness/docs/idea-summaries/06-who-self-improvement-leaves-behind.md:245)

3. **High — The Global-MMLU gate has no specified valid test and may reject even identical checkpoints.** `line_start` 140 · `line_end` 156 · confidence 0.99. Fresh items address adaptive reuse. The guarantee still requires a conditionally valid per-test procedure, which the proposal leaves unspecified. With a 3-point margin and 50 equal spends, a null with harm `+1` on 3% of items and zero elsewhere produces 112 ties with probability 3.3%. A deterministic test at level 0.001 therefore cannot pass an all-tie sample even at E6’s largest size. **Recommendation** Specify the finite-sample paired test and shortlist one candidate before opening each fresh batch. Derive power for that test at realistic rates of changed answers, then resize the sweep or narrow the claim. [Proposal](/Users/theodoremui/dev/bioharness/docs/idea-summaries/06-who-self-improvement-leaves-behind.md:140)

